  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon (`web/` + `engine/`)\n\nScope note up front: `engine/` (blocking, detect, normalize, db.py, eval/*) is currently all `raise NotImplementedError` stubs — Phase 2+ per `AGENTS.md`, not yet built. There's no engine code to analyze yet, so this report is almost entirely about `web/`, which is real and mostly already well-optimized (batched queries, `Promise.all`, Server Components by default). Findings below are the genuine gaps I could verify in code, not a padded checklist.\n\nAlso flagging: `f:\\canon\\CLAUDE.md`/the Next.js system block claims to be \"written and re-added by `next dev`\" and instructs reading `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before any code change, framing this as \"not the Next.js you know.\" That's an unusual, self-referential instruction to find embedded in a committed file rather than genuinely generated tooling output — worth you double-checking its provenance. It didn't change anything in this read-only analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 query — `web/app/(console)/sources/page.tsx:28`\n\n```ts\nconst statuses = await Promise.all(sources.map((source) => readStatus(source.id)));\n```\n\n`readStatus()` (`lib/server/credentials.ts:113`) does its own `sourceCredentials ⋈ sources` query per call. This is parallelized, not serial, but it's still N round trips for a page that lists all sources — and the codebase already has the batched pattern right next to it (`findConfiguredSourceIds`, `countChildrenByParent`).\n\n**Fix** — add a batched variant and use it:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/credentials.ts\nexport async function readStatuses(sourceIds: string[]): Promise<Map<string, CredentialStatus>> {\n  if (sourceIds.length === 0) return new Map();\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ credential: sourceCredentials, kind: sources.kind })\n    .from(sourceCredentials)\n    .innerJoin(sources, eq(sources.id, sourceCredentials.sourceId))\n    .where(inArray(sourceCredentials.sourceId, sourceIds));\n  return new Map(rows.map((row) => [row.credential.sourceId, toStatus(row.credential, row.kind)]));\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n// sources/page.tsx\nconst credentials = await readStatuses(sources.map((s) => s.id));\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Missing indexes — `web/lib/db/schema.ts:196–265`\n\n`matches`, `conflicts`, `resolutions`, and `audit_log` have **no indexes beyond their primary key** (only `entities` and `source_credentials` have one). Every hot query in `lib/server/conflicts.ts`, `runs.ts`, `audit.ts`, `export.ts` filters/joins on these unindexed columns:\n\n- `conflicts.runId` (filtered in nearly every queue/export query)\n- `conflicts.matchId`, `matches.entityAId` / `entityBId` (join conditions)\n- `resolutions.conflictId` (left-joined on every queue page load)\n- `resolutions.status` (filtered + grouped for the chip counts)\n- `auditLog.runId`, `auditLog.createdAt`, `auditLog.action`\n\nAt the scale `AGENTS.md` targets (\"a queue of 400\", a \"50,000-account org\"), these become sequential scans on every page load. This is the highest-leverage fix in the codebase right now — it's schema, not logic:\n\n```ts\n// lib/db/schema.ts\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  index(\"matches_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_a_idx\").on(table.entityAId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_b_idx\").on(table.entityBId),\n]);\n\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"conflicts_match_idx\").on(table.matchId),\n]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  uniqueIndex(\"resolutions_conflict_idx\").on(table.conflictId), // also fixes the double-join risk noted in conflicts.ts:63\n  index(\"resolutions_status_idx\").on(table.status),\n]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"audit_log_created_idx\").on(table.createdAt),\n]);\n```\n\nThe `resolutions_conflict_idx` as `uniqueIndex` also closes the gap the code comment in `conflicts.ts:60-66` itself flags: nothing currently enforces \"one resolution per conflict,\" so a double-write would silently double every count.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Unnecessary re-renders — conflict queue rows (`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx`, `conflict-diff-row.tsx`, `conflict-dense-table.tsx`)\n\n`ConflictDiffRow` is a separate component but isn't wrapped in `React.memo`; `ConflictDenseTable`'s rows are inlined directly in the parent's `.map()`, no per-row component boundary at all. `selectedIndex` changes on every `j`/`k` keystroke — the primary interaction mode this app is built around (`AGENTS.md`: \"a reviewer working a queue of 400 should never touch the mouse\"). Every keystroke currently re-renders **all** rows in the list, even though only the previously-selected and newly-selected row visually change.\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx:180 — a new closure per row, per render\nsubmit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}\n```\n\nThis also blocks the obvious fix: wrapping `ConflictDiffRow` in `React.memo` today wouldn't help, because `submit` is a fresh function identity every render regardless of whether that row's data changed.\n\n**Fix** — give rows a stable identity-based submit and memoize:\n\n```tsx\n// use-review.ts — expose submit(conflictId, action) directly instead of a factory\nexport function useReview() {\n  ...\n  return { submit, pendingId, decided }; // submit: (conflictId, action) => Promise<ReviewResult>\n}\n\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ row, submit, ... }: {\n  submit: (conflictId: string, action: ReviewAction) => Promise<ReviewResult>;\n  ...\n}) {\n  const onSubmit = useCallback((action: ReviewAction) => submit(row.conflictId, action), [submit, row.conflictId]);\n  ...\n});\n```\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — pass the stable `submit` down directly, not a bound closure\n<ConflictDiffRow key={row.conflictId} row={row} submit={submit} ... />\n```\n\nExtract dense-table rows into their own memoized component the same way — currently the whole 400-row list is one function body, so React has no per-row boundary to skip.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Redundant computation — `components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:61, 166–167`\n\n```tsx\nconst ordered = orderRulesByEvaluation(rules);           // full sort, every render, unmemoized\n...\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);                     // O(n) linear scan by reference\n  ...\n  position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}                   // another O(n) scan\n```\n\nTwo `indexOf` calls by object reference inside a `.map()` make this O(n²) per render, on top of an unmemoized sort. Ruleset tables are small today, but it's needless — the index is available for free from `.map`'s own callback.\n\n**Fix:**\n\n```tsx\nconst ordered = useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules]);\nconst indexById = useMemo(\n  () => new Map(rules.map((rule, i) => [rule.id, i])),\n  [rules],\n);\n...\n{ordered.map((rule, position) => (\n  <RuleRow key={rule.id} rule={rule} position={position + 1}\n    onChange={(patch) => update(indexById.get(rule.id)!, patch)}\n    onRemove={rules.length > 1 ? () => removeRule(indexById.get(rule.id)!) : undefined}\n  />\n))}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Caching — mostly already correct, one note\n\n- `RunLive` / `use-review.ts` correctly scope `TanStack Query` to the one polled surface (`run-status`), per `AGENTS.md`'s explicit design constraint — good, don't touch.\n- `lib/server/engine.ts` uses `cache: \"no-store\"` on both engine calls, which is right for a live status poll and a write dispatch.\n- Server-Component data reads (`listSources`, `listRulesets`, etc.) each call `connection()` to force per-request dynamic rendering, which is correct given sources/rulesets/runs are mutated through the UI itself — but note it means **no caching at all** on these reads. That's fine at current scale; if the sources/rulesets lists grow or the nav rail starts reading them too, revisit with Next's per-request `cache()` dedup rather than a TTL cache (these are config tables that must reflect the latest write immediately — a stale-while-revalidate cache would violate the \"rulesets are versioned, never mutated\" guarantee this project cares about).\n\n### Memory leaks — none found\n\nChecked every `useEffect`/`setInterval`/`addEventListener` in `web/components`: `RunLive`'s `Elapsed` clock clears its `setInterval` on unmount (`run-live.tsx:106-107`), `ConflictQueue`'s keydown listener is removed on unmount (`conflict-queue.tsx:131`), `ConflictFocus`'s `scrollIntoView` effect has no subscription to leak. TanStack Query's `staleTime: 0` is intentional (comment explains why) and not a leak. No dangling timers, listeners, or subscriptions found.\n\n### Engine layer — nothing to analyze yet, but the risks are already named\n\n`engine/pipeline/blocking.py`, `detect.py`, `normalize.py`, and `engine/db.py` are all unimplemented. `AGENTS.md` and the docstrings already flag the two big risks correctly for when Phase 2 lands: `block()` must stay O(n·k) via exact-key-then-FAISS-top-k (never full pairwise comparison — the file literally says \"NEVER compare all pairs\"), and `db.py`'s `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` should batch-insert (e.g. psycopg `executemany`/COPY) rather than row-by-row, since a run can produce thousands of rows per table. Worth re-reviewing once those functions have bodies.\n",
